//! The conformance kit: one small Go reference program per `cir/1` feature, its `cir/1` module, the
//! calls to make and the output they give, and the runner that checks all of it
//! (`docs/conformance.md` §§ 2, 3).
//!
//! # Layout
//!
//! ```text
//! conformance/
//!   kit.json                 codespec-conformance-kit/1, generated by `codespec-conformance kit`
//!   driver/go/main.go        the reference driver: prints each call's results
//!   cases/<case>/
//!     case.go                the reference program, package `<case>` with `-` read as `_`
//!     case.cir.json          case.go read by codespec-read-go and run through `codespec pass`
//!     calls.json             codespec-calls/1: the calls to make, in order
//!     expected.txt           what the reference prints for those calls
//!     features.json          codespec-case-features/1: the features the case exercises
//!     java/Case.java         optional: the case in Java, package `<case>` with `-` read as `_`
//! ```
//!
//! A case directory holds exactly these five files, and may hold a `java/` directory, which
//! this crate does not read: the Java reader's tests run it through `codespec read --lang java`,
//! the passes, the Go writer and `codespec-run-go` against `expected.txt`
//! (`adapters/java/test/codespec/read/ReaderTest.java`). `case.cir.json` and `expected.txt` are
//! generated (`codespec-conformance check --write`), never edited by hand; the other three are
//! written by hand.
//!
//! # `kit.json`: `codespec-conformance-kit/1`
//!
//! [`kit_json`] builds it from `schemas/cir-1.features.json`, read strictly by
//! [`features_from_json`]: `format`, `cir` (the vocabulary's `cir`, `cir/1`) and `features`, the
//! vocabulary's feature objects unchanged. Two-space indentation, a final newline.
//!
//! # `features.json`: `codespec-case-features/1`
//!
//! | field | type | meaning |
//! |---|---|---|
//! | `format` | string | always `codespec-case-features/1` |
//! | `features` | array of feature ids | every feature the case's module uses, each once; the first is the case's **subject**, the rest in id order |
//! | `edges` | array of strings, optional | for a semantic subject, the edges its calls cross, by name: `MaxInt64 + 1`, `-7 % 3` |
//!
//! "Uses" is [`features_used`] of `case.cir.json`, and the declared set must equal it, so a case
//! cannot claim a feature its module does not contain nor hide one it does.
//!
//! # `calls.json`: `codespec-calls/1`
//!
//! ```json
//! {"format": "codespec-calls/1", "calls": [{"func": "Add", "args": ["9223372036854775807", "1"]}]}
//! ```
//!
//! `func` names an exported top-level function of the case (no receiver). Each argument is one
//! value in the harness's value encoding, as a JSON string:
//!
//! | parameter type | argument |
//! |---|---|
//! | `int`, any width | a decimal integer: `-` for a negative, no leading zero, within `int64` |
//! | `bool` | `true` or `false` |
//! | `string` | a double-quoted string of printable ASCII other than `"` and `\` |
//! | `chan` (both directions or send-only) of a printable type | `chan`: a fresh channel with room for 64 values, drained after the call |
//!
//! The argument count and kinds must match the function's parameters in `case.cir.json`; anything
//! else is refused. `calls.json` and `features.json` are canonical: two-space indentation, a final
//! newline.
//!
//! # Output
//!
//! Each call prints one line, after anything the function prints itself (`intrinsic.println`):
//!
//! | the function returns | line |
//! |---|---|
//! | nothing | `ok` |
//! | values, the last not an `error` | `ok <v> <v> …` |
//! | values ending in an `error` that is not nil | `err <Error()>` |
//! | values ending in a nil `error` | `ok <v> …` (the values before the error) |
//!
//! then one line `sent <v>` per value sent on each channel argument, in order. A value is an
//! integer in decimal, `true` or `false`, a string as Go's `strconv.Quote` writes it, or a slice as
//! `[` its elements separated by one space `]`; a declared newtype or enum prints as its underlying
//! value and an `any` as the value it holds. Other result types (unsigned integers, structs,
//! pointers, maps) are refused when the calls are generated: an entry function returns plain values.
//!
//! # The Go driver
//!
//! One fixed program, `conformance/driver/go/main.go`, serves every case. Go cannot call a
//! function by name, so [`calls_go`] generates `calls.go`, which makes each call of `calls.json` in
//! its own block, with its arguments as Go literals (or fresh channels), hands the results to the
//! driver's `none`, `values` or `fallible`, chosen from the function's result types in
//! `case.cir.json`, and then `drain`s each channel. [`run_reference`] copies the driver, the
//! generated `calls.go` and `case.go` (as package `codespec.conformance/case/subject`) into
//! `<build>/`, writes a `go.mod`, builds with `go build` (`GOWORK=off`, `GOFLAGS` cleared) and runs
//! the binary.
//!
//! The runners of written code reuse this: `codespec-run-go` (`adapters/go/cmd/codespec-run-go`)
//! embeds the same driver and generates the same `calls.go`, taking each signature from the
//! written package's `go/types` instead of `case.cir.json`; `codespec-run-rust`
//! (`adapters/rust-run`) prints the same lines from a generated `main.rs`. Both check every
//! argument with [`check_argument`] or its Go copy and read `calls.json` as [`read_calls`] does.
//! The acceptance test of `adapters/rust-run` holds them to it on every case.
//!
//! This follows the examples' drivers (`examples/*/drivers/go`: a `main` that calls the package
//! under test and prints one line per command) but not their trace on stdin, since a case's calls
//! are fixed and can be compiled in, and not their `go build -overlay`, since a build directory of
//! real files is simpler to inspect when a case fails.
//!
//! # Checking a case
//!
//! [`check_case`] reads `case.go` with `codespec read --lang go` (through `codespec-read-go`),
//! runs `codespec pass` on it and compares the bytes with `case.cir.json`; then runs the reference
//! on `calls.json` and compares its output with `expected.txt`, reporting the first differing line;
//! then checks `features.json` against the module. With `write`, the two generated files are
//! rewritten instead of compared. `codespec-conformance check` runs every case this way, several
//! at a time.
//!
//! A case that declares `sem.map.order` has its reference run [`MAP_ORDER_RUNS`] times, and output
//! that differs between runs is refused: its `expected.txt` must not depend on iteration order.
//!
//! # Semantic features
//!
//! [`features_used`] names a semantic feature where the module contains the construct whose
//! meaning two languages disagree on:
//!
//! | id | where |
//! |---|---|
//! | `sem.int.wrap` | an integer `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `<<`, unary `-`, `incr`, `decr`, or `+=`, `-=`, `*=`, `/=`, `<<=` |
//! | `sem.rem.sign` | `%` or `%=` |
//! | `sem.continue.post` | a `continue` whose innermost loop is a `for` with a `post` |
//! | `sem.multi_assign.order` | an `assign` without `op` with more than one target |
//! | `sem.map.order` | a `for_range` over a map |
//! | `sem.switch.break` | a `break` whose innermost enclosing loop or switch is a switch |

use std::collections::BTreeSet;
use std::fmt::Write as _;
use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};
use std::process::Command;

use codespec_model::{Expr, Feature, Module, Stmt, Type, TypeDecl, features_from_json, vocabulary};
use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};
use serde_json::Value;

/// The format of `conformance/kit.json`.
pub const KIT_FORMAT: &str = "codespec-conformance-kit/1";
/// The format of a case's `features.json`.
pub const CASE_FEATURES_FORMAT: &str = "codespec-case-features/1";
/// The format of a case's `calls.json`.
pub const CALLS_FORMAT: &str = "codespec-calls/1";
/// The files a case directory holds, and no others but the optional [`CASE_SOURCE_DIRS`].
pub const CASE_FILES: &[&str] = &[
    "calls.json",
    "case.cir.json",
    "case.go",
    "expected.txt",
    "features.json",
];

/// The directories a case may hold besides [`CASE_FILES`]: the case in another language, read by
/// that language's reader tests and never by this crate.
pub const CASE_SOURCE_DIRS: &[&str] = &["java"];

/// Why the kit or a case is not what this crate defines; the text names the file and the fault.
pub type Error = String;

/// `conformance/kit.json`.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]
pub struct Kit {
    pub format: String,
    pub cir: String,
    pub features: Vec<Feature>,
}

/// The `codespec-conformance-kit/1` document for a `codespec-features/1` vocabulary: two-space
/// indentation, a final newline. The vocabulary is read strictly.
pub fn kit_json(features: &[u8]) -> Result<String, Error> {
    let doc = features_from_json(features).map_err(|e| format!("feature vocabulary: {e}"))?;
    let kit = Kit {
        format: KIT_FORMAT.to_string(),
        cir: doc.cir,
        features: doc.features,
    };
    let mut s = serde_json::to_string_pretty(&kit).expect("a kit always serializes");
    s.push('\n');
    Ok(s)
}

/// A case's `features.json`.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]
pub struct CaseFeatures {
    pub format: String,
    pub features: Vec<String>,
    #[serde(default, skip_serializing_if = "Vec::is_empty")]
    pub edges: Vec<String>,
}

/// One call of `calls.json`.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]
pub struct Call {
    pub func: String,
    pub args: Vec<String>,
}

/// A case's `calls.json`.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]
pub struct Calls {
    pub format: String,
    pub calls: Vec<Call>,
}

/// One case directory, read and validated by [`load_case`].
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct Case {
    pub name: String,
    pub dir: PathBuf,
    pub features: CaseFeatures,
    pub calls: Calls,
    pub module: Module,
}

impl Case {
    /// The feature the case exists for: the first of `features.json`.
    pub fn subject(&self) -> &str {
        &self.features.features[0]
    }
}

fn read(path: &Path) -> Result<Vec<u8>, Error> {
    std::fs::read(path).map_err(|e| format!("{}: {e}", path.display()))
}

/// The canonical encoding of a hand-written case file: two-space indentation, a final newline.
pub fn canonical(v: &impl Serialize) -> String {
    let mut s = serde_json::to_string_pretty(v).expect("a case file always serializes");
    s.push('\n');
    s
}

/// Read a hand-written case file of `format`, refusing unknown fields, another format and an
/// encoding other than [`canonical`].
fn strict<T: for<'de> Deserialize<'de> + Serialize>(path: &Path, format: &str) -> Result<T, Error> {
    let bytes = read(path)?;
    let probe: Value =
        serde_json::from_slice(&bytes).map_err(|e| format!("{}: {e}", path.display()))?;
    let found = probe.get("format").and_then(Value::as_str).unwrap_or("");
    if found != format {
        return Err(format!(
            "{}: format is {found:?}, this reader reads {format:?}",
            path.display()
        ));
    }
    let doc: T = serde_json::from_value(probe).map_err(|e| format!("{}: {e}", path.display()))?;
    if canonical(&doc).as_bytes() != bytes {
        return Err(format!(
            "{}: not canonical (two-space indentation, a final newline)",
            path.display()
        ));
    }
    Ok(doc)
}

/// The Go package name of a case: its directory name with `-` read as `_`.
pub fn package_name(case: &str) -> String {
    case.replace('-', "_")
}

/// Read one case directory, refusing a missing or extra file, a module that is not canonical
/// `cir/1`, a package name other than the case's, a feature list that is empty, repeats an id,
/// names an id the vocabulary does not have or is not in id order after its subject, and calls
/// that do not fit the module ([`calls_go`]).
pub fn load_case(dir: &Path) -> Result<Case, Error> {
    let name = dir
        .file_name()
        .and_then(|n| n.to_str())
        .ok_or_else(|| format!("{}: not a case directory", dir.display()))?
        .to_string();
    let mut files: Vec<String> = std::fs::read_dir(dir)
        .map_err(|e| format!("{}: {e}", dir.display()))?
        .map(|e| e.map(|e| e.file_name().to_string_lossy().into_owned()))
        .collect::<Result<_, _>>()
        .map_err(|e| format!("{}: {e}", dir.display()))?;
    files.sort();
    files.retain(|f| !(CASE_SOURCE_DIRS.contains(&f.as_str()) && dir.join(f).is_dir()));
    if files != CASE_FILES {
        return Err(format!(
            "{name}: holds {files:?}, a case holds exactly {CASE_FILES:?}"
        ));
    }
    let features: CaseFeatures = strict(&dir.join("features.json"), CASE_FEATURES_FORMAT)?;
    let calls: Calls = strict(&dir.join("calls.json"), CALLS_FORMAT)?;
    let bytes = read(&dir.join("case.cir.json"))?;
    let module = Module::from_json(&bytes).map_err(|e| format!("{name}/case.cir.json: {e}"))?;
    if module.to_json().as_bytes() != bytes {
        return Err(format!("{name}/case.cir.json is not canonical"));
    }
    if module.name != package_name(&name) {
        return Err(format!(
            "{name}: package {:?}, a case's package is {:?}",
            module.name,
            package_name(&name)
        ));
    }
    let known: BTreeSet<String> = vocabulary().into_iter().map(|f| f.id).collect();
    let Some((_, rest)) = features.features.split_first() else {
        return Err(format!("{name}/features.json: no features"));
    };
    let mut seen = BTreeSet::new();
    for id in &features.features {
        if !known.contains(id) {
            return Err(format!("{name}/features.json: unknown feature {id:?}"));
        }
        if !seen.insert(id) {
            return Err(format!("{name}/features.json: {id:?} twice"));
        }
    }
    if rest.windows(2).any(|w| w[0] >= w[1]) {
        return Err(format!(
            "{name}/features.json: the features after the subject are not in id order"
        ));
    }
    let case = Case {
        name,
        dir: dir.to_path_buf(),
        features,
        calls,
        module,
    };
    calls_go(&case.module, &case.calls).map_err(|e| format!("{}/calls.json: {e}", case.name))?;
    Ok(case)
}

/// Every case under `cases_dir`, sorted by name.
pub fn load_cases(cases_dir: &Path) -> Result<Vec<Case>, Error> {
    let mut dirs: Vec<PathBuf> = std::fs::read_dir(cases_dir)
        .map_err(|e| format!("{}: {e}", cases_dir.display()))?
        .map(|e| e.map(|e| e.path()))
        .collect::<Result<_, _>>()
        .map_err(|e| format!("{}: {e}", cases_dir.display()))?;
    dirs.sort();
    dirs.iter().map(|d| load_case(d)).collect()
}

fn k(v: &impl Serialize) -> String {
    let v = serde_json::to_value(v).expect("a cir/1 node always serializes");
    v["k"]
        .as_str()
        .expect("every tagged node has a `k`")
        .to_string()
}

/// The feature id of an operator: the `op.`, `unop.` or `assign.` feature whose `op` is `op`.
fn operator_id(vocab: &[Feature], prefix: &str, op: &str) -> Result<String, Error> {
    vocab
        .iter()
        .find(|f| f.id.starts_with(prefix) && f.op.as_deref() == Some(op))
        .map(|f| f.id.clone())
        .ok_or_else(|| format!("operator {op:?} has no {prefix} feature"))
}

#[derive(Clone, Copy)]
enum Breakable {
    Loop { post: bool },
    Switch,
}

struct Walk {
    vocab: Vec<Feature>,
    types: Vec<TypeDecl>,
    out: BTreeSet<String>,
    breakable: Vec<Breakable>,
}

impl Walk {
    /// An integer, or a declared newtype or enum over one.
    fn is_int(&self, t: &Type) -> bool {
        match t {
            Type::Int { .. } => true,
            Type::Named { name } => self.types.iter().any(|d| match d {
                TypeDecl::Newtype { name: n, under, .. }
                | TypeDecl::Enum { name: n, under, .. } => {
                    n == name && matches!(under, Type::Int { .. })
                }
                _ => false,
            }),
            _ => false,
        }
    }

    fn add(&mut self, id: impl Into<String>) {
        self.out.insert(id.into());
    }

    fn ty(&mut self, t: &Type) {
        self.add(format!("type.{}", k(t)));
        match t {
            Type::Ptr { elem } | Type::Slice { elem } | Type::Chan { elem, .. } => self.ty(elem),
            Type::Map { key, elem } => {
                self.ty(key);
                self.ty(elem);
            }
            Type::Tuple { elems } => elems.iter().for_each(|e| self.ty(e)),
            Type::Int { .. }
            | Type::Bool
            | Type::String
            | Type::Error
            | Type::Any
            | Type::Unit
            | Type::Named { .. } => {}
        }
    }

    fn decl(&mut self, d: &TypeDecl) -> Result<(), Error> {
        self.add(format!("decl.{}", k(d)));
        match d {
            TypeDecl::Struct { fields, .. } => fields.iter().for_each(|f| self.ty(&f.t)),
            TypeDecl::Newtype { under, .. } => self.ty(under),
            TypeDecl::Enum {
                under, variants, ..
            } => {
                self.ty(under);
                for v in variants {
                    self.expr(&v.value)?;
                }
            }
            TypeDecl::Union { variants, .. } => {
                for v in variants {
                    v.fields.iter().for_each(|f| self.ty(&f.t));
                }
            }
        }
        Ok(())
    }

    fn exprs(&mut self, es: &[Expr]) -> Result<(), Error> {
        es.iter().try_for_each(|e| self.expr(e))
    }

    fn expr(&mut self, e: &Expr) -> Result<(), Error> {
        self.add(format!("expr.{}", k(e)));
        self.ty(e.t());
        match e {
            Expr::Lit { .. }
            | Expr::Nil { .. }
            | Expr::Var { .. }
            | Expr::Const { .. }
            | Expr::Errvar { .. }
            | Expr::Make { .. }
            | Expr::Zero { .. } => {}
            Expr::Field { x, .. } | Expr::Conv { x, .. } | Expr::ToError { x, .. } => {
                self.expr(x)?
            }
            Expr::Index { x, index, .. } => {
                self.expr(x)?;
                self.expr(index)?;
            }
            Expr::Call { args, .. } => self.exprs(args)?,
            Expr::Method { x, args, .. } => {
                self.expr(x)?;
                self.exprs(args)?;
            }
            Expr::Binop { op, x, y, t } => {
                let id = operator_id(&self.vocab, "op.", op)?;
                self.add(id);
                if self.is_int(t) && ["+", "-", "*", "/", "<<"].contains(&op.as_str()) {
                    self.add("sem.int.wrap");
                }
                if op == "%" {
                    self.add("sem.rem.sign");
                }
                self.expr(x)?;
                self.expr(y)?;
            }
            Expr::Unop { op, x, t } => {
                let id = operator_id(&self.vocab, "unop.", op)?;
                self.add(id);
                if self.is_int(t) && op == "-" {
                    self.add("sem.int.wrap");
                }
                self.expr(x)?;
            }
            Expr::Struct { fields, .. } => {
                for f in fields {
                    self.expr(&f.value)?;
                }
            }
            Expr::Intrinsic { name, args, .. } => {
                let id = format!("intrinsic.{name}");
                if !self.vocab.iter().any(|f| f.id == id) {
                    return Err(format!("intrinsic {name:?} has no feature"));
                }
                self.add(id);
                self.exprs(args)?;
            }
        }
        Ok(())
    }

    fn body(&mut self, b: &[Stmt]) -> Result<(), Error> {
        b.iter().try_for_each(|s| self.stmt(s))
    }

    fn inside(&mut self, b: Breakable, body: &[Stmt]) -> Result<(), Error> {
        self.breakable.push(b);
        let r = self.body(body);
        self.breakable.pop();
        r
    }

    fn stmt(&mut self, s: &Stmt) -> Result<(), Error> {
        self.add(format!("stmt.{}", k(s)));
        match s {
            Stmt::Let { rhs, .. } => self.exprs(rhs)?,
            Stmt::Var { t, .. } => self.ty(t),
            Stmt::Assign { op, lhs, rhs, .. } => {
                if op.is_empty() {
                    if lhs.len() > 1 {
                        self.add("sem.multi_assign.order");
                    }
                } else {
                    let id = operator_id(&self.vocab, "assign.", op)?;
                    self.add(id);
                    let int = lhs.first().is_some_and(|x| self.is_int(x.t()));
                    if int && ["+=", "-=", "*=", "/=", "<<="].contains(&op.as_str()) {
                        self.add("sem.int.wrap");
                    }
                    if op == "%=" {
                        self.add("sem.rem.sign");
                    }
                }
                self.exprs(lhs)?;
                self.exprs(rhs)?;
            }
            Stmt::Incr { x, .. } | Stmt::Decr { x, .. } => {
                if self.is_int(x.t()) {
                    self.add("sem.int.wrap");
                }
                self.expr(x)?;
            }
            Stmt::If {
                cond,
                then,
                otherwise,
                ..
            } => {
                self.expr(cond)?;
                self.body(then)?;
                self.body(otherwise)?;
            }
            Stmt::ForRange { over, body, .. } => {
                if matches!(over.t(), Type::Map { .. }) {
                    self.add("sem.map.order");
                }
                self.expr(over)?;
                self.inside(Breakable::Loop { post: false }, body)?;
            }
            Stmt::For {
                init,
                cond,
                post,
                body,
                ..
            } => {
                if let Some(i) = init {
                    self.stmt(i)?;
                }
                if let Some(c) = cond {
                    self.expr(c)?;
                }
                if let Some(p) = post {
                    self.stmt(p)?;
                }
                let has_post = post.is_some();
                self.inside(Breakable::Loop { post: has_post }, body)?;
            }
            Stmt::Break { .. } => {
                if matches!(self.breakable.last(), Some(Breakable::Switch)) {
                    self.add("sem.switch.break");
                }
            }
            Stmt::Continue { .. } => {
                let innermost = self.breakable.iter().rev().find_map(|b| match b {
                    Breakable::Loop { post } => Some(*post),
                    Breakable::Switch => None,
                });
                if innermost == Some(true) {
                    self.add("sem.continue.post");
                }
            }
            Stmt::Switch { tag, cases, .. } => {
                if let Some(t) = tag {
                    self.expr(t)?;
                }
                for c in cases {
                    self.exprs(&c.values)?;
                    self.inside(Breakable::Switch, &c.body)?;
                }
            }
            Stmt::Return { results, .. } => self.exprs(results)?,
            Stmt::Expr { x, .. } => self.expr(x)?,
            Stmt::Send { chan, x, .. } => {
                self.expr(chan)?;
                self.expr(x)?;
            }
            Stmt::LookupElse {
                map, index, then, ..
            } => {
                self.expr(map)?;
                self.expr(index)?;
                self.body(then)?;
            }
            Stmt::Foreign { .. } => {}
        }
        Ok(())
    }
}

/// Every feature id a module uses: the `k` of each statement, expression, type and type
/// declaration; the operator, compound assignment and intrinsic of each node that carries one;
/// each fact whose list is not empty; and the semantic features of the crate doc's table. An
/// operator or intrinsic the vocabulary has no id for is an error.
pub fn features_used(m: &Module) -> Result<BTreeSet<String>, Error> {
    let mut w = Walk {
        vocab: vocabulary(),
        types: m.types.clone(),
        out: BTreeSet::new(),
        breakable: Vec::new(),
    };
    for d in &m.types {
        w.decl(d)?;
    }
    for c in &m.consts {
        w.ty(&c.t);
        w.expr(&c.value)?;
    }
    for f in &m.funcs {
        if let Some(r) = &f.recv {
            w.ty(&r.t);
        }
        f.params.iter().for_each(|p| w.ty(&p.t));
        f.results.iter().for_each(|t| w.ty(t));
        w.body(&f.body)?;
    }
    if let Some(facts) = &m.facts {
        let facts = serde_json::to_value(facts).expect("facts always serialize");
        for (name, v) in facts.as_object().expect("facts are an object") {
            if v.as_array().is_some_and(|a| !a.is_empty()) {
                w.add(format!("fact.{name}"));
            }
        }
    }
    Ok(w.out)
}

/// The features one top-level function of a module uses in its own signature and body, as
/// [`features_used`] names them, without facts or type declarations. An unknown function is an
/// error.
pub fn features_used_by(m: &Module, func: &str) -> Result<BTreeSet<String>, Error> {
    let f = m
        .funcs
        .iter()
        .find(|f| f.name == func && f.recv.is_none())
        .ok_or_else(|| format!("no function {func:?}"))?;
    let mut w = Walk {
        vocab: vocabulary(),
        types: m.types.clone(),
        out: BTreeSet::new(),
        breakable: Vec::new(),
    };
    f.params.iter().for_each(|p| w.ty(&p.t));
    f.results.iter().for_each(|t| w.ty(t));
    w.body(&f.body)?;
    Ok(w.out)
}

/// How the driver prints a function's results.
fn shape(results: &[Type], m: &Module) -> Result<&'static str, Error> {
    let Some((last, init)) = results.split_last() else {
        return Ok("none");
    };
    let (values, fallible) = match last {
        Type::Error => (init, true),
        _ => (results, false),
    };
    for t in values {
        printable(t, m)?;
    }
    Ok(if fallible { "fallible" } else { "values" })
}

fn printable(t: &Type, m: &Module) -> Result<(), Error> {
    match t {
        Type::Int { signed: false, .. } => Err(
            "an unsigned result cannot be printed: the driver prints signed integers only".into(),
        ),
        Type::Int { .. } | Type::Bool | Type::String | Type::Any => Ok(()),
        Type::Slice { elem } => printable(elem, m),
        Type::Named { name } => match m.types.iter().find(|d| d.name() == name) {
            Some(TypeDecl::Newtype { under, .. } | TypeDecl::Enum { under, .. }) => {
                printable(under, m)
            }
            _ => Err(format!("result type {name} is not a newtype or enum")),
        },
        other => Err(format!("result type {} cannot be printed", k(other))),
    }
}

/// The Go spelling of a channel element type in the driver.
fn go_type(t: &Type, m: &Module) -> Result<String, Error> {
    printable(t, m)?;
    Ok(match t {
        Type::Int { width, .. } => match width {
            codespec_model::IntWidth::Platform => "int".to_string(),
            codespec_model::IntWidth::W8 => "int8".to_string(),
            codespec_model::IntWidth::W16 => "int16".to_string(),
            codespec_model::IntWidth::W32 => "int32".to_string(),
            codespec_model::IntWidth::W64 => "int64".to_string(),
        },
        Type::Bool => "bool".to_string(),
        Type::String => "string".to_string(),
        Type::Any => "any".to_string(),
        Type::Slice { elem } => format!("[]{}", go_type(elem, m)?),
        Type::Named { name } => format!("subject.{name}"),
        other => return Err(format!("type {} has no driver spelling", k(other))),
    })
}

/// How one `calls.json` argument reaches a parameter of type `t`: a Go literal, or for a channel
/// parameter (argument `chan`) a fresh buffered channel the driver drains after the call.
enum Arg {
    Literal(String),
    Chan(String),
}

/// The kinds of parameter a `calls.json` argument can be given to; every runner checks an
/// argument against its parameter with [`check_argument`].
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum ArgKind {
    Int,
    Bool,
    String,
    Chan,
}

impl ArgKind {
    fn name(self) -> &'static str {
        match self {
            ArgKind::Int => "int",
            ArgKind::Bool => "bool",
            ArgKind::String => "string",
            ArgKind::Chan => "chan",
        }
    }
}

/// Whether `arg` is a value of `kind` in the encoding of the crate doc's `calls.json` table. An
/// integer that passes fits `i64`; a string that passes is the same text as a Go, Rust or Java
/// string literal.
pub fn check_argument(arg: &str, kind: ArgKind) -> Result<(), Error> {
    let ok = match kind {
        ArgKind::Int => {
            let digits = arg.strip_prefix('-').unwrap_or(arg);
            !digits.is_empty()
                && digits.bytes().all(|b| b.is_ascii_digit())
                && (digits == "0" || !digits.starts_with('0'))
                && arg != "-0"
                && arg.parse::<i64>().is_ok()
        }
        ArgKind::Bool => arg == "true" || arg == "false",
        ArgKind::String => {
            arg.len() >= 2
                && arg.starts_with('"')
                && arg.ends_with('"')
                && arg[1..arg.len() - 1]
                    .bytes()
                    .all(|b| (b' '..=b'~').contains(&b) && b != b'"' && b != b'\\')
        }
        ArgKind::Chan => {
            if arg != "chan" {
                return Err(format!(
                    "argument {arg:?} for a channel parameter is not `chan`"
                ));
            }
            true
        }
    };
    if ok {
        Ok(())
    } else {
        Err(format!("argument {arg:?} is not a {} value", kind.name()))
    }
}

/// Read a `calls.json` strictly, as [`load_case`] does: unknown fields, another format and an
/// encoding other than [`canonical`] are refused, and so is a list of no calls.
pub fn read_calls(path: &Path) -> Result<Calls, Error> {
    let calls: Calls = strict(path, CALLS_FORMAT)?;
    if calls.calls.is_empty() {
        return Err(format!("{}: no calls", path.display()));
    }
    Ok(calls)
}

fn argument(arg: &str, t: &Type, m: &Module) -> Result<Arg, Error> {
    let kind = match t {
        Type::Int { .. } => ArgKind::Int,
        Type::Bool => ArgKind::Bool,
        Type::String => ArgKind::String,
        Type::Chan { elem, dir } => {
            if *dir == codespec_model::ChanDir::Recv {
                return Err("a receive-only channel parameter cannot be passed".into());
            }
            check_argument(arg, ArgKind::Chan)?;
            return Ok(Arg::Chan(go_type(elem, m)?));
        }
        other => return Err(format!("parameter type {} cannot be passed", k(other))),
    };
    check_argument(arg, kind).map_err(|_| format!("argument {arg:?} is not a {} value", k(t)))?;
    Ok(Arg::Literal(arg.to_string()))
}

/// The capacity of each channel the driver passes; a case sends fewer values than this per call.
pub const CHAN_CAPACITY: usize = 64;

/// The driver's `calls.go` for a case: `func run()` making each call of `calls` in order, each in
/// its own block. Refuses a call of a function the module does not export at top level, a wrong
/// argument count or form, and a result type the driver cannot print.
pub fn calls_go(m: &Module, calls: &Calls) -> Result<String, Error> {
    if calls.calls.is_empty() {
        return Err("no calls".into());
    }
    let mut s = String::from("// Code generated from calls.json. DO NOT EDIT.\n\npackage main\n\n");
    s.push_str("import subject \"codespec.conformance/case/subject\"\n\nfunc run() {\n");
    for c in &calls.calls {
        let f = m
            .funcs
            .iter()
            .find(|f| f.name == c.func && f.recv.is_none() && f.exported)
            .ok_or_else(|| format!("no exported function {:?}", c.func))?;
        if f.params.len() != c.args.len() {
            return Err(format!(
                "{} takes {} arguments, the call passes {}",
                c.func,
                f.params.len(),
                c.args.len()
            ));
        }
        let mut args = Vec::new();
        let mut chans = Vec::new();
        s.push_str("\t{\n");
        for (i, (a, p)) in c.args.iter().zip(&f.params).enumerate() {
            match argument(a, &p.t, m).map_err(|e| format!("{}: {e}", c.func))? {
                Arg::Literal(l) => args.push(l),
                Arg::Chan(elem) => {
                    let name = format!("ch{i}");
                    writeln!(s, "\t\t{name} := make(chan {elem}, {CHAN_CAPACITY})")
                        .expect("writing to a String");
                    args.push(name.clone());
                    chans.push(name);
                }
            }
        }
        let args = args.join(", ");
        match shape(&f.results, m).map_err(|e| format!("{}: {e}", c.func))? {
            "none" => writeln!(s, "\t\tsubject.{}({args})\n\t\tnone()", c.func),
            how => writeln!(s, "\t\t{how}(subject.{}({args}))", c.func),
        }
        .expect("writing to a String");
        for ch in chans {
            writeln!(s, "\t\tdrain({ch})").expect("writing to a String");
        }
        s.push_str("\t}\n");
    }
    s.push_str("}\n");
    Ok(s)
}

fn run(cmd: &mut Command, what: &str) -> Result<String, Error> {
    let out = cmd
        .output()
        .map_err(|e| format!("{what}: cannot start: {e}"))?;
    if !out.status.success() {
        return Err(format!(
            "{what}: {}\n{}{}",
            out.status,
            String::from_utf8_lossy(&out.stdout),
            String::from_utf8_lossy(&out.stderr)
        ));
    }
    String::from_utf8(out.stdout).map_err(|e| format!("{what}: output is not UTF-8: {e}"))
}

fn absolute(p: &Path) -> Result<PathBuf, Error> {
    std::path::absolute(p).map_err(|e| format!("{}: {e}", p.display()))
}

/// Build the reference of a case with the Go toolchain in `build` (emptied first) and return what
/// it prints for the case's calls. `driver` is the directory holding the driver's `main.go`.
pub fn run_reference(case: &Case, driver: &Path, build: &Path) -> Result<String, Error> {
    run_reference_stable(case, driver, build, 1)
}

/// How often [`check_case`] runs the reference of a case that declares `sem.map.order`.
pub const MAP_ORDER_RUNS: usize = 100;

/// Build the reference once as [`run_reference`] does and run it `runs` times, refusing output
/// that differs between runs: a case whose output depends on the order of a map loop would make
/// its `expected.txt` a matter of luck.
pub fn run_reference_stable(
    case: &Case,
    driver: &Path,
    build: &Path,
    runs: usize,
) -> Result<String, Error> {
    let build = absolute(build)?;
    if build.exists() {
        std::fs::remove_dir_all(&build).map_err(|e| format!("{}: {e}", build.display()))?;
    }
    let subject = build.join("subject");
    std::fs::create_dir_all(&subject).map_err(|e| format!("{}: {e}", subject.display()))?;
    let write = |p: PathBuf, bytes: &[u8]| {
        std::fs::write(&p, bytes).map_err(|e| format!("{}: {e}", p.display()))
    };
    write(
        build.join("go.mod"),
        b"module codespec.conformance/case\n\ngo 1.22\n",
    )?;
    write(build.join("main.go"), &read(&driver.join("main.go"))?)?;
    write(
        build.join("calls.go"),
        calls_go(&case.module, &case.calls)?.as_bytes(),
    )?;
    write(subject.join("case.go"), &read(&case.dir.join("case.go"))?)?;
    let bin = build.join("reference");
    run(
        Command::new("go")
            .args(["build", "-o"])
            .arg(&bin)
            .arg(".")
            .current_dir(&build)
            .env("GOWORK", "off")
            .env("GOFLAGS", ""),
        &format!("{}: go build", case.name),
    )?;
    let what = format!("{}: reference", case.name);
    let first = run(&mut Command::new(&bin), &what)?;
    for i in 2..=runs {
        let again = run(&mut Command::new(&bin), &what)?;
        if let Some(d) = first_difference(&first, &again) {
            return Err(format!(
                "{what}: output differs between runs (run 1 and run {i}, line {}: {:?} and {:?}); \
                 its output must not depend on map iteration order",
                d.line, d.expected, d.got
            ));
        }
    }
    Ok(first)
}

/// The first line at which `got` differs from `expected`, 1-based; a missing line is empty.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct Difference {
    pub line: usize,
    pub expected: String,
    pub got: String,
}

/// Where two outputs first differ, or `None` when they are byte-equal.
pub fn first_difference(expected: &str, got: &str) -> Option<Difference> {
    if expected == got {
        return None;
    }
    let (e, g): (Vec<&str>, Vec<&str>) = (
        expected.split_inclusive('\n').collect(),
        got.split_inclusive('\n').collect(),
    );
    let n = e.len().max(g.len());
    let line = (0..n)
        .find(|&i| e.get(i) != g.get(i))
        .expect("unequal outputs differ at some line");
    let at = |v: &[&str]| {
        v.get(line)
            .map_or("", |l| l.trim_end_matches('\n'))
            .to_string()
    };
    Some(Difference {
        line: line + 1,
        expected: at(&e),
        got: at(&g),
    })
}

/// The programs [`check_case`] runs.
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct Tools {
    /// The `codespec` binary.
    pub codespec: PathBuf,
    /// The directory holding `codespec-read-go`.
    pub adapters: PathBuf,
    /// The directory holding the driver's `main.go`.
    pub driver: PathBuf,
}

fn compare(case: &str, file: &str, expected: &str, got: &str) -> Result<(), Error> {
    match first_difference(expected, got) {
        None => Ok(()),
        Some(d) => Err(format!(
            "{case}/{file}: line {} expected {:?}, got {:?}",
            d.line, d.expected, d.got
        )),
    }
}

/// Check one case as the crate doc says, working in `build/<case>`; with `write`, rewrite
/// `case.cir.json` and `expected.txt` from the tools instead of comparing with them. The declared
/// features are checked against the module in both modes.
pub fn check_case(case: &Case, tools: &Tools, build: &Path, write: bool) -> Result<(), Error> {
    let work = build.join(&case.name);
    std::fs::create_dir_all(&work).map_err(|e| format!("{}: {e}", work.display()))?;
    let (read_out, passed) = (work.join("read.cir.json"), work.join("passed.cir.json"));
    run(
        Command::new(&tools.codespec)
            .args(["read", "--lang", "go"])
            .arg(&case.dir)
            .arg("--out")
            .arg(&read_out)
            .arg("--adapters")
            .arg(&tools.adapters),
        &format!("{}: codespec read", case.name),
    )?;
    run(
        Command::new(&tools.codespec)
            .arg("pass")
            .arg(&read_out)
            .arg("--out")
            .arg(&passed),
        &format!("{}: codespec pass", case.name),
    )?;
    let module_bytes = String::from_utf8(read(&passed)?)
        .map_err(|e| format!("{}: passed module is not UTF-8: {e}", case.name))?;
    let module = Module::from_json(module_bytes.as_bytes())
        .map_err(|e| format!("{}: passed module: {e}", case.name))?;
    let checked = Case {
        module,
        ..case.clone()
    };
    let runs = if case.features.features.iter().any(|f| f == "sem.map.order") {
        MAP_ORDER_RUNS
    } else {
        1
    };
    let output = run_reference_stable(&checked, &tools.driver, &work.join("go"), runs)?;
    let cir_path = case.dir.join("case.cir.json");
    let expected_path = case.dir.join("expected.txt");
    if !write {
        let checked_in = String::from_utf8(read(&cir_path)?)
            .map_err(|e| format!("{}: case.cir.json is not UTF-8: {e}", case.name))?;
        compare(&case.name, "case.cir.json", &checked_in, &module_bytes)?;
        let expected = String::from_utf8(read(&expected_path)?)
            .map_err(|e| format!("{}: expected.txt is not UTF-8: {e}", case.name))?;
        compare(&case.name, "expected.txt", &expected, &output)?;
    }
    let used = features_used(&checked.module).map_err(|e| format!("{}: {e}", case.name))?;
    let declared: BTreeSet<String> = case.features.features.iter().cloned().collect();
    if used != declared {
        return Err(format!(
            "{}/features.json: declares {:?}, the module uses {:?}",
            case.name,
            declared.difference(&used).collect::<Vec<_>>(),
            used.difference(&declared).collect::<Vec<_>>()
        ));
    }
    if write {
        std::fs::write(&cir_path, &module_bytes)
            .map_err(|e| format!("{}: {e}", cir_path.display()))?;
        std::fs::write(&expected_path, &output)
            .map_err(|e| format!("{}: {e}", expected_path.display()))?;
    }
    Ok(())
}

/// Read an adapter's `codespec-adapter/1` manifest strictly (`codespec_model::manifest`) and
/// return its claim; with `read`, the modules a reader produced, every claimed feature must be one
/// at least one of them uses as [`features_used`] names it, so the claim holds only what the
/// reader was seen to read. The error names every claimed feature none of them uses.
pub fn check_manifest(bytes: &[u8], read: &[&Module]) -> Result<BTreeSet<String>, Error> {
    let m = codespec_model::manifest_from_json(bytes).map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;
    let claimed = m.claimed();
    if read.is_empty() {
        return Ok(claimed);
    }
    let mut used = BTreeSet::new();
    for module in read {
        used.extend(features_used(module)?);
    }
    let missing: Vec<&String> = claimed.difference(&used).collect();
    if missing.is_empty() {
        Ok(claimed)
    } else {
        Err(format!(
            "claimed, and no module read uses them: {missing:?}"
        ))
    }
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    fn module(body: &str) -> Module {
        let doc = format!(
            r#"{{"format":"cir/1","name":"t","reader":{{"language":"go","name":"r","version":"0"}},
            "types":[],"funcs":[{{"name":"F","exported":true,"params":[],"results":[],
            "body":{body},"pos":"t.go:1"}}],"layout":[]}}"#
        );
        Module::from_json(doc.as_bytes()).unwrap()
    }

    const INT: &str = r#"{"k":"int","width":"platform","signed":true}"#;

    #[test]
    fn a_continue_in_a_for_with_a_post_uses_sem_continue_post_and_a_range_does_not() {
        let cont = r#"{"k":"continue","pos":"t.go:2"}"#;
        let incr =
            format!(r#"{{"k":"incr","x":{{"k":"var","name":"i","t":{INT}}},"pos":"t.go:2"}}"#);
        let with_post = module(&format!(
            r#"[{{"k":"for","post":{incr},"body":[{cont}],"pos":"t.go:2"}}]"#
        ));
        assert!(
            features_used(&with_post)
                .unwrap()
                .contains("sem.continue.post")
        );
        let over = format!(r#"{{"k":"var","name":"s","t":{{"k":"slice","elem":{INT}}}}}"#);
        let range = module(&format!(
            r#"[{{"k":"for_range","key":"_","val":"_","over":{over},"body":[{cont}],"pos":"t.go:2"}}]"#
        ));
        let used = features_used(&range).unwrap();
        assert!(!used.contains("sem.continue.post"), "{used:?}");
        assert!(!used.contains("sem.map.order"), "{used:?}");
    }

    #[test]
    fn a_break_directly_inside_a_switch_uses_sem_switch_break() {
        let brk = r#"{"k":"break","pos":"t.go:3"}"#;
        let m = module(&format!(
            r#"[{{"k":"for","body":[{{"k":"switch","cases":[{{"values":[],"body":[{brk}]}}],"pos":"t.go:2"}}],"pos":"t.go:1"}}]"#
        ));
        assert!(features_used(&m).unwrap().contains("sem.switch.break"));
        let plain = module(&format!(r#"[{{"k":"for","body":[{brk}],"pos":"t.go:1"}}]"#));
        assert!(!features_used(&plain).unwrap().contains("sem.switch.break"));
    }

    #[test]
    fn an_operator_outside_the_vocabulary_is_an_error() {
        let x = format!(r#"{{"k":"var","name":"a","t":{INT}}}"#);
        let m = module(&format!(
            r#"[{{"k":"expr","x":{{"k":"binop","op":"**","x":{x},"y":{x},"t":{INT}}},"pos":"t.go:2"}}]"#
        ));
        assert!(features_used(&m).unwrap_err().contains("\"**\""));
    }

    #[test]
    fn a_kit_from_an_unknown_vocabulary_format_is_refused() {
        let err = kit_json(br#"{"format":"codespec-features/2","cir":"cir/1","features":[]}"#)
            .unwrap_err();
        assert!(err.contains("codespec-features/2"), "{err}");
    }

    #[test]
    fn calls_with_an_unknown_field_or_a_malformed_argument_are_refused() {
        let bad: Result<Calls, _> = serde_json::from_str(
            r#"{"format":"codespec-calls/1","calls":[{"func":"F","args":[],"extra":1}]}"#,
        );
        assert!(bad.is_err());
        let m = module("[]");
        let int = Type::Int {
            width: codespec_model::IntWidth::Platform,
            signed: true,
        };
        for a in ["01", "-0", "+1", "9223372036854775808", "1.0", ""] {
            assert!(argument(a, &int, &m).is_err(), "{a}");
        }
        assert!(argument("-9223372036854775808", &int, &m).is_ok());
        for a in [r#""a\"b""#, r#""a\\b""#, "\"\u{e9}\"", "abc"] {
            assert!(argument(a, &Type::String, &m).is_err(), "{a}");
        }
    }

    fn manifest(features: &str) -> Vec<u8> {
        format!(
            r#"{{"format":"codespec-adapter/1","role":"reader","lang":"x","version":"1","cir":"cir/1","features":{features}}}"#
        )
        .into_bytes()
    }

    /// The kit's Go driver prints signed integers only (`show`): an unsigned result is refused when
    /// `calls.go` is generated, not by the driver after the build.
    #[test]
    fn an_unsigned_result_is_refused_when_the_calls_are_generated() {
        let doc = r#"{"format":"cir/1","name":"t","reader":{"language":"go","name":"r","version":"0"},
            "types":[],"funcs":[{"name":"F","exported":true,"params":[],
            "results":[{"k":"int","width":"64","signed":false}],
            "body":[],"pos":"t.go:1"}],"layout":[]}"#;
        let m = Module::from_json(doc.as_bytes()).unwrap();
        let calls = Calls {
            format: CALLS_FORMAT.into(),
            calls: vec![Call {
                func: "F".into(),
                args: vec![],
            }],
        };
        let err = calls_go(&m, &calls).expect_err("an unsigned result is refused");
        assert!(err.contains("unsigned"), "{err}");
    }

    #[test]
    fn a_reader_claim_is_covered_only_by_modules_that_use_each_claimed_feature() {
        let brk = module(r#"[{"k":"for","body":[{"k":"break","pos":"t.go:2"}],"pos":"t.go:1"}]"#);
        let claimed = check_manifest(&manifest(r#"["stmt.for", "stmt.break"]"#), &[&brk])
            .unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!("{e}"));
        assert_eq!(
            claimed.into_iter().collect::<Vec<_>>(),
            ["stmt.break", "stmt.for"]
        );
        let err = check_manifest(&manifest(r#"["stmt.for", "stmt.switch"]"#), &[&brk])
            .expect_err("stmt.switch is not covered");
        assert!(
            err.contains("stmt.switch") && !err.contains("stmt.for"),
            "{err}"
        );
        let unknown = check_manifest(&manifest(r#"["stmt.goto"]"#), &[])
            .expect_err("an unknown id is refused");
        assert!(unknown.contains("stmt.goto"), "{unknown}");
        let foreign = module(
            r#"[{"k":"foreign","lang":"go","src":"defer f()","reason":"r","pos":"t.go:2"}]"#,
        );
        let only_foreign = check_manifest(&manifest(r#"["stmt.foreign"]"#), &[&foreign]);
        assert!(only_foreign.is_ok(), "{only_foreign:?}");
    }
}
